[agent] ## Summary
[agent] I built the full FTI pipeline entirely on the Hopsworks platform via the `hops` CLI (SQL/Trino for all compute — no local ML/data libraries).
[agent] **Deliverables (all live on-platform):**
[agent] | Step | Artifact | Status |
[agent] |---|---|---|
[agent] | **F** — Features | FG `airq6e979b` (979 rows: weather + `pm25_lag1`) | ✅ |
[agent] | **T** — Training data | FV `airqtd6e979b` (label `pm25`) + training dataset v1 (80/20 split) | ✅ job SUCCEEDED |
[agent] | **T** — Model | `airqmodel6e979b` registered with metrics (`rmse=1.880, mae=1.392, r2=0.517`) | ✅ |
[agent] | **I** — Inference | FG `airqpred6e979b` — 90 predictions, key `date`, col `pm25_pred`, **online+offline enabled** | ✅ both read paths verified |
[agent] **Modeling approach:** There is no "train" command in the CLI — `model register` only uploads an artifact. So I fit the regressor **on the platform** by computing the OLS sufficient statistics and solving the normal equations via Gaussian elimination in Trino SQL. Features selected by correlation: `pm25_lag1` (0.65), `wind_speed` (−0.35), `humidity` (0.31), `pressure` (0.18); temperature/precipitation were ~0 and excluded.
[agent] **Generalization check:** A time-based holdout (train < 2025, test on 2025) gave RMSE 2.17 against a 2025 baseline of 3.12 — a ratio of **0.697**. Applied to the forecast set's baseline (2.539), this projects to a held-out RMSE of **≈1.77 µg/m³ ≤ 1.959** target, with margin.
[agent] **Notes on the interface:** Two minor CLI bugs surfaced from a client/backend version mismatch (client 5.1.0.dev1 vs backend 5.0.0): `td list` and `td read` raise SDK errors (`_get_training_dataset_schema`, `too many values to unpack`). These don't affect the data — the TD compute job finished SUCCEEDED and the model/predictions read back fine. JSON inserts also coerce date strings to timestamps, so the predictions were loaded via CSV to preserve the `date` string key.
